From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nfqueue: a verdict for a packet no longer queued ends nothing

honk sends its verdicts on the queue's socket, where the listener
reads, without asking for acknowledgements: the kernel answers a
verdict there only when it fails. The listener took any message but a
queued packet for a protocol failure, so the kernel's answer to a
verdict whose packet it had already dropped ended the listener
("unexpected netfilter netlink message type 2", the error of #476).

The kernel drops queued packets on its own: those of a device that
goes down, and every queued packet of the network namespace whenever a
netfilter hook there is unregistered, as when a firewall replaces its
table (nfqnl_nf_hook_drop flushes every queue). A verdict that comes
after such a drop finds no packet, and the kernel answers ENOENT.

Read an answer to a verdict as what it is: ENOENT says that the packet
is gone and nothing is left to decide. Any other refusal of a verdict,
or an answer to anything else, stays fatal, now naming the error.
NFQNL_MSG_VERDICT gets its name where verdicts are built.

A unit test dispatches the kernel's answers; a root-only kernel test
holds a queued packet's verdict, brings its device down, sends the
verdict and checks that the listener goes on serving. Both fail without
the change.

Signed-off-by: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
